SAR ADC split capacitive DAC, one half (x18 of the golden deck), ideal capacitors, 1024 codes in one run
* specs/analog/decks/sar_cdac_half.cir -- test data for specs/analog/sar_cdac.t27 (#10540). A plain netlist, no .control block.
* Topology of Diff_cap_DAC (the golden SAR ADC deck, subckt Diff_cap_DAC, lines 529-591): 31 unit capacitors from net1 (node l) to the LSB plates
* p1..p5 (1, 2, 4, 8, 16 units), one unit between net1 and Vout (node m), 31 unit capacitors from Vout to the MSB plates p6..p10 (1, 2, 4, 8, 16).
* Unit = 100 fF, ideal. Plate p_j is Bit_j. The tracking switch is not modelled: Vout and net1 start at the sampled 0.75 V (.ic, and IC=0 on every
* capacitor because every plate rests at VCM = 0.75 V and the bridge is uncharged). From 10 ns, interval n (n = 0..1023, 10 ns each) puts plate j
* at VDD = 1.5 V when bit j-1 of n is 1 and at 0 V when it is 0: the code is n, and the plates go straight from code n-1 to code n. The network is
* algebraic (capacitors only), so the settled voltage of an interval does not depend on the time grid; a B source declares no breakpoint.
* Read the LAST printed point inside each interval. Run: ngspice -b sar_cdac_half.cir   (.print shows 7 digits; the spec's table was read with
* set numdgt=15 in a control block wrapped around this same netlist, so it holds the full float64 values).
